%I A399760 #60 Nov 05 2026 14:20:42
%S A399760 3,29,13,15,3,7,125739576223134033,17653185166242129,3,59,13,
%T A399760 8645985911501137,3,7,27,2220427886710549,3,258899142758165,13,121,3,
%U A399760 7,3812445114133,31,3,513910230805,13,258985749,3,7,109,8145,3,29,13,15,3,7,239032323861,515861
%N A399760 Odd part of the parity vector of 4*n-1 under the reduced Collatz map A014682 until its trajectory first goes below 4*n-1, read in binary, with a 1 for each (3*x+1)/2 step and a 0 for each x/2 step.
%C A399760 The parity vector of x until the drop ends with at least one 0, as the drop is an x/2 step; leaving out those final 0's gives the odd part. For x = 7 the parity vector is 1110100, 116 in decimal, with odd part 11101, so a(2) = 29.
%C A399760 Left out are the even x and the x == 1 (mod 4). For even x the parity vector is 0, as a single halving drops an even, so there is no odd part. For x == 1 (mod 4) the parity vector is 10, odd part 1 (for x = 1 the trajectory 1, 2, 1 returns to 1 instead of going below it). Interleaved with A000012 (all 1's), as 1, a(1), 1, a(2), 1, a(3), ..., the terms give the odd part of the parity vector of every odd 2*n-1.
%C A399760 A000120(a(n)) is the number of (3*x+1)/2 steps, and the parity vector has A126241(4*n-1) steps. Terras's coefficient stopping time conjecture (Terras 1976, Conjecture 2.9) says that every x > 1 goes below x at the first step k with 2^k > 3^j, j the number of (3*x+1)/2 steps among the first k steps. It implies A126241(4*n-1) = A020914(A000120(a(n))), so the number of final 0's follows from a(n), and a(n) determines the parity vector. Terras proved the conjecture for k < 2593, Garner for k < 105000.
%C A399760 All x == 4*n-1 (mod 2^L), L = A126241(4*n-1), have the same parity vector until the drop, so the terms repeat with period 2^(L-2) (see Formula), and each distinct term first occurs at a 4*n-1 in A243115. The drop value goes up by 3^A000120(a(n)) for each step of 2^L in x. Examples:
%C A399760   (5+0*3^4)/(7+0*2^7) = 5/7, and (3*(3*(3*(3*(7)+1)/2+1)/2+1)/2/2+1)/2/2/2 = 5, so drop(7) = 5;
%C A399760   (5+1*3^4)/(7+1*2^7) = 86/135, and (3*(3*(3*(3*(135)+1)/2+1)/2+1)/2/2+1)/2/2/2 = 86, so drop(135) = 86.
%H A399760 Ruud H.G. van Tol, <a href="/A399760/b399760.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399760 Lynn E. Garner, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9939-1981-0603593-2">On the Collatz 3n+1 algorithm</a>, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 82 (1981), 19-22.
%H A399760 Riho Terras, <a href="https://doi.org/10.4064/aa-30-3-241-252">A stopping time problem on the positive integers</a>, Acta Arithmetica 30 (1976), pp. 241-252.
%H A399760 <a href="/index/3#3x1">Index entries for sequences related to 3x+1 (or Collatz) problem</a>
%F A399760 a(n) = a(n + 2^(A126241(4*n-1)-2)).
%F A399760 The binary expansion of a(n) starts with A001511(n)+1 1's.
%e A399760 a(n) = 3 for n == 1 (mod 4), because x = 4*n-1 = 16*i+3 and
%e A399760   16*i+3 -> (3*(16*i+3)+1)/2 = 24*i+5 -> (3*(24*i+5)+1)/2 = 36*i+8 -> 18*i+4 -> 9*i+2 (the drop),
%e A399760 so the parity vector is 1100, with odd part 11 in binary, 3 in decimal.
%e A399760 Initial terms:
%e A399760    n   x   d  a(n)  binary
%e A399760    1:  3;  2;  3;   11
%e A399760    2:  7;  5; 29;   11101
%e A399760    3: 11; 10; 13;   1101
%e A399760    4: 15; 10; 15;   1111
%e A399760    5: 19; 11;  3;   11
%e A399760    6: 23; 20;  7;   111
%e A399760    7: 27; 23; 125739576223134033;
%e A399760               110111110101101110111101001110110111111001111000101010001
%e A399760    8: 31; 23; 17653185166242129;
%e A399760               111110101101110111101001110110111111001111000101010001
%e A399760    9: 35; 20;  3;   11
%e A399760   10: 39; 38; 59;   111011
%e A399760   [...]
%e A399760 Here x = 4*n-1 and d is the first value of its trajectory below x.
%o A399760 (PARI) a(n) = my(x=n*4-1, d=x, v=0); until(d<x, d=if(d%2, v=2*v+1; 3*d+1, v*=2; d)/2); v>>valuation(v, 2);
%Y A399760 Cf. A000012, A000120, A001511, A014682, A020914, A050603, A094267, A100982, A126241, A136480, A243115, A389685.
%K A399760 nonn,new
%O A399760 1,1
